<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1180–c. 1253</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodore Komnenos Doukas (c. 1180–c. 1253) was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> from 1215 who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> in 1224 and had himself crowned emperor. His Empire of Thessalonica rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> for the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> until he was defeated and captured by the Bulgarian tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> at Klokotnitsa in 1230. He was blinded in captivity but later regained influence through his sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas.jpg" alt="Theodore Komnenos Doukas" width="320" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: [1] (cropped and uploaded by Odysses) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodore_Comnenus-Ducas_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theodore was the son of the sebastokrator John Doukas and Zoe Doukaina, and a great-grandson of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> through his paternal grandmother Theodora. The emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-angelos">Alexios III Angelos</a> were his first cousins. He preferred the surname Doukas or Komnenos Doukas to Angelos, and the hostile later historians Nikephoros Gregoras and Theodore Skoutariotes were the only medieval writers to call him Angelos.</p><p>Nothing is known of his life before the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1204, after which he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore I Laskaris</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> for some years before being called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, where his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-i-komnenos-doukas">Michael I Komnenos Doukas</a> had founded a principality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Michael was assassinated in late 1214 or 1215, and Theodore sidelined Michael's young illegitimate son Michael II and took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. He allied himself with the Serbian prince Stefan II Nemanjić, expanded into Macedonia, and captured the Latin Emperor Peter II of Courtenay in 1217. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> fell to him in 1224.</p><p>He claimed the imperial title, and since the Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> refused to crown him, because the Patriarch at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> was regarded as legitimate, a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> at Arta in March 1225 recognised only him as emperor and Archbishop Demetrios Chomatianos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> crowned him. That year he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, advanced through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and blockaded Adrianople. His attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was delayed until 1230, when he marched against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> instead and was defeated and captured by John Asen II at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klokotnitsa">Battle of Klokotnitsa</a> in April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Theodore was freed in 1237, when his daughter Irene married John Asen II. He re-entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> disguised as a beggar and ousted his brother Manuel. Having been blinded, he could not hold the throne, so he installed his son John, and ruled in his name. In 1241 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, and Theodore was in effect detained there.</p><p>Vatatzes annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1246, and in 1252 forced Theodore's nephew Michael II to terms and took Theodore prisoner. He died in exile around 1253. The biographer Konstantinos Varzos judged that his ambition to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and his rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> delayed the restoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> by several decades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theodore Komnenos Doukas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Despotate of Epirus under <b>Theodore Komnenos Doukas</b> captured and executed Peter of Courtenay, took Thessalonica in 1224 and conquered Thrace in 1225–26, reaching Constantinople itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivan Asen II (r. 1218–1241) won the Battle of Klokotnitsa on 9 March 1230, capturing <b>Theodore Komnenos Doukas</b>, ruler of Epirus and Thessalonica, and bringing much of Thrace, Macedonia and Albania under Bulgarian rule, as he proclaimed in an inscription in the Church of the Forty Martyrs in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tsar Ivan Asen II of Bulgaria routed and captured <b>Theodore Komnenos Doukas</b>, ruler of Epirus and Thessalonica, who had broken a treaty and invaded Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-9">9 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Theodore Komnenos Doukas</b>, ruler of the Despotate of Epirus, steadily conquered the Kingdom of Thessalonica and captured the city in 1224.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-thessalonica">Empire of Thessalonica</a></li><li><blockquote>Weakened by internal divisions and Bulgarian attacks, the kingdom was gradually conquered by <b>Theodore Komnenos Doukas</b> of Epirus, who took Thessalonica itself in 1224 and founded the Empire of Thessalonica.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-thessalonica">Kingdom of Thessalonica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.64,22.94&amp;year=1227" aria-label="Thessaloniki on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5112 712 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Theodore Komnenos Doukas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5562 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5562 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.64,22.94&amp;year=1227">What was here in 1227? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
